From: EglantinePS5 <noreply@eglantineps5.local>
Subject: [PATCH] Migrate 0210-split-wait-report.patch

Source: 0210-split-wait-report.patch
Moved by hand: the map drain site is set at upstream's EDEN_NX2_MAP_ON_BACK form of the run command; the profile switch re-read is upstream's 0053 already, those hunks dropped

gpu_split: report where the front waits for the back (EDEN_NX2_SPLIT_WAITS=1)

Core patch. Paths are the submodule's. Numbered after 0181; 0210 to 0216 go in order.

Source: zortos293/Lichii, GPL-3.0-or-later. DrainSite and the shared wait come from nx2 at
27226d2568 (src/video_core/gpu_split.cpp, nx2_profile.h), carried in PR 161 (999sian, 64bb6fe0d7)
and PR 179 (tibzejoker, head 3ce0ab7106). The report line is ours: the core's own zones cost ~25% of
the GPU thread on the console (docs/MKW-TITLE-FPS.md), so it reads clocks only around waits.

Why: run61 cannot say which waits cost time. Its only split log fires after 5 s, and it never fired.

EDEN_NX2_SPLIT_WAITS=1 logs every 10 s from the front:
  GPU split per second: back idle N ms, N ring words, front waited N ms, acquire N ms (N/s, longest
  N ms), list writers ..., macro dma read ...
Back idle near 0 means the back is the limit. Back idle high with little front wait means it is not:
the guest or the front's own parsing is, and this line cannot tell which. Idle time includes the idle
hook's submits.

Also here:
- WaitForSpace and WaitForWriters now share one wait. WaitForWriters had no re-wake and no report;
  it now has both, as Drain has. Drain() itself is untouched (rebase-02 changes it); the new
  Drain(site) wraps it.
- EDEN_NX2_GPU_PROFILE was a global initialised from getenv, which runs before main reads env.txt,
  so by reading the code it could never be turned on from env.txt. It is read on the GPU thread now.
  Not proven: docs/MKW-TITLE-FPS.md gives a cost measured on the console, and no log here shows it.

Runs on the PS5: yes. Run61 logs the split and macros on the first thread.
Behaviour with the switch off: same waits and order. A full ring waits as it did: 4096 spins and no
wait_target. With the switch on it spins longer and sets wait_target, so the back hands space back
sooner. WaitForWriters' re-wake and 5 s warning are always there, and act only in a wait that is stuck.
Hang: cannot add one. No new wait; one more place re-wakes a parked back.
Pool memory: under 1 KiB.
Checked: gpu_split.cpp built on the Mac with stubs and run under ThreadSanitizer, 2 sessions, front
and back; PS5-target syntax compile. Not run on the console.

